/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A commutator, a rotating switch in the classic picture and a multiplexer chip today, visits the N channels in turn and takes one sample from each per revolution.
   - All channels should be bandlimited by their own anti-aliasing filters before the commutator.
   - In PAM-TDM each slot holds one analog sample.
   - N data channels of n bits each, plus S framing and signalling bits per frame, at fs frames per second.
   - E1 uses slot 0 for frame alignment and alarms and slot 16 for signalling, leaving 30 speech slots.
   - A single sync bit per frame is a weak marker, because data bits at the same position can imitate it for a while; T1 therefore spreads a fixed pattern over 12 or 24 frames, and E1 sends a 7-bit alignment word in slot 0 of alternate frames.
   - If the receiver samples early it reads the tail of the previous slot; if late, the start of the next.
*/
